# Managed by Ansible (roles/hardening). AIDE rules for logs lamware adds to the host,
# and for rotated copies.
#
# Without these, the logs below fall to `/ 0 Full` (99_aide_root): every append
# is a change and every rotation is a set of adds, removes and changes. The active
# logs that logrotate rotates are in 30_lamware_rotated_logs, which must sort
# before Ubuntu's 31_aide_* (AIDE uses the earliest file's rule).
#
# Every rule here must hold against a baseline of any age: lamware-aide refreshes
# the database only after a deploy or a package install, not nightly.

# Process accounting (acct): /etc/cron.daily/acct runs `savelog -c 30` at 06:25:
# pacct -> pacct.0 -> pacct.1.gz -> ... -> pacct.29.gz (dropped next day).
# Ubuntu's chain rules (ActLog/RotLog/...SerLog) assume AIDE's database is
# refreshed every night, so each copy is compared with its own yesterday. Ours
# is refreshed only after a deploy or a package install, often days earlier, so
# the live file had shrunk and the numbered copies had shifted names (checks of
# 2026-11-01 and 11-04). Only owner and mode hold against a baseline of any age.
/var/log/account$ d VarDir
/var/log/account/pacct(\.0)?$ f FreqRotLog
/var/log/account/pacct\.[0-9]+\.@@{LOGEXT}$ f LowLog+ANF+ARF

# sudo's log (Defaults logfile, sudoers.d/012_logfile): appended, not rotated.
/var/log/sudo\.log$ f ActLog

# PostgreSQL: weekly copytruncate (so the active file shrinks in place), dateext,
# delaycompress with xz: main.log-YYYYMMDD for a week, then main.log-YYYYMMDD.xz.
/var/log/postgresql$ d VarDir
/var/log/postgresql/postgresql-[0-9]+-main\.log$ f FreqRotLog
/var/log/postgresql/postgresql-[0-9]+-main\.log-[0-9]{8}(\.xz)?$ f LowDELog

# Not a log, but the same kind of rule: nuclei (security-test) creates and removes
# entries in /root on every run, which changes the directory's mtime. VarDir
# drops the times and keeps owner, mode and inode; everything inside /root stays
# on Full (an added or changed file there is still reported).
/root$ d VarDir

# Every logrotate-managed log on this host rotates with `dateext` and xz
# (/etc/logrotate.conf), so a rotated copy is name-YYYYMMDD, then
# name-YYYYMMDD.xz a cycle later (delaycompress). Ubuntu's package rules
# (31_aide_*) expect `.1` / `.2.gz` names, which this host never writes, so
# rotated copies fell to Full and every rotation was a set of adds and removes.
# Measured 2026-10-26 00:00 (the first real night): nginx's rotation. A rotated
# copy may appear and disappear; once written, its content must not change.
/var/log/.+-[0-9]{8}(\.xz)?$ f LowDELog

# A rotation creates and removes entries, which changes each log directory's times.
/var/log(/.+)?$ d VarDir

# lamware's own append-only logs, never rotated: they may grow, not shrink or change
# owner. Appended nightly (digest, retention) or every 5 minutes (network monitor).
/opt/ntfy-alerts/digest\.log$ f ActLog
/opt/CAPEv2/log/retention\.log$ f ActLog
/opt/network-monitor/network-monitor\.log$ f ActLog

# certbot runs twice a day and touches /etc/letsencrypt's own times each run.
# Only the directory: a renewal that changes a certificate or key under it is
# still reported (about every 60 days).
/etc/letsencrypt$ d VarDir

